messages.content:
Review this newly written event-reconciliation example. It describes a hypothetical warehouse service, not an existing software package.

Each event has an item name, a positive integer revision, an operation, and a value. Operations are either set or delete. A set value is a nonnegative integer quantity. Delete has no value. Events can arrive in any order. For each item, the event with the greatest revision determines its final state. Repeating an identical event must have no effect. Two different events for the same item and revision are invalid input and must raise an error, even if a newer revision has already arrived. A deletion must prevent an older set from restoring the item.

The proposed implementation is:

```python
def reconcile(events):
    current = {}
    for item, revision, operation, value in events:
        previous = current.get(item)
        if previous is not None and revision < previous[0]:
            continue
        if operation == 'delete':
            current.pop(item, None)
        else:
            current[item] = (revision, value)
    return {item: value for item, (_, value) in current.items()}
```

The author tested only streams whose revisions increase for each item. They also tested repeating the final set event. Both tests passed, so they believe the implementation satisfies the contract. Another engineer observes that a service restart may replay older events after a deletion. A third points out that the final quantities are not enough to determine whether the input contained a conflict.

Explain the distinct correctness defects and give a compact corrected Python implementation. State what information it must retain while consuming the stream and why. Include a short example demonstrating an older set after a deletion, and another demonstrating a conflicting old revision after a newer revision. Do not add persistence, networking, concurrency, or external libraries. You may assume all event fields have valid types and operations.
